1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,857
Coach: Try the same listening approach in a new discussion.

2
00:00:03,857 --> 00:00:08,571
Coach: A group is planning a short guide for new club members.

3
00:00:08,571 --> 00:00:12,000
Coach: They want it to be easy to read

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,571
Coach: and useful to someone who does not already know how the club works.

5
00:00:17,571 --> 00:00:19,714
Coach: No version has been chosen.

6
00:00:20,064 --> 00:00:23,493
Maya: A shorter guide would be quicker to read,

7
00:00:23,493 --> 00:00:27,350
Maya: but some new members may not know the terms.

8
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:31,986
Coach: Separate the benefit Maya suggests from the concern she raises.

9
00:00:31,986 --> 00:00:37,128
Coach: Do not turn the possibility that some new members may not know

10
00:00:37,128 --> 00:00:41,414
Coach: the terms into a claim that they cannot understand anything.

11
00:00:41,414 --> 00:00:46,557
Coach: Do not assume she wants a longer guide unless she says so.

12
00:00:46,907 --> 00:00:49,907
Coach: What would be useful to say next?

13
00:00:49,907 --> 00:00:53,336
Coach: You could check your understanding, clarify an uncertainty,

14
00:00:53,336 --> 00:00:57,193
Coach: support a specific point, or add a relevant suggestion.

15
00:00:57,193 --> 00:01:02,764
Coach: Choose one move that fits this moment and form a short reply privately.

16
00:01:15,114 --> 00:01:20,257
You: Which terms do you think need a short explanation for new members?

17
00:01:20,607 --> 00:01:24,035
Maya: The names of our activity groups, for example.

18
00:01:24,035 --> 00:01:28,321
Maya: A new member might not know what each one does.

19
00:01:28,671 --> 00:01:31,671
Coach: The question makes the uncertainty more specific.

20
00:01:31,671 --> 00:01:35,957
Coach: The group can now discuss how to explain those names.

21
00:01:35,957 --> 00:01:40,671
Coach: That does not settle the length or design of the guide.

22
00:01:40,671 --> 00:01:45,814
Coach: Another relevant reply might also have helped; this is one authored example.

23
00:01:46,164 --> 00:01:50,021
Coach: Review a reply you formed, if you formed one.

24
00:01:50,021 --> 00:01:53,878
Coach: Did you keep the person's meaning and uncertainty intact?

25
00:01:53,878 --> 00:01:57,735
Coach: Did your response connect to the issue they raised?

26
00:01:57,735 --> 00:02:02,021
Coach: Pick one feature you would like to check next time.

27
00:02:10,371 --> 00:02:16,371
Coach: You do not have to paraphrase, ask, agree and add something in every turn.

28
00:02:16,371 --> 00:02:19,371
Coach: Listening helps you choose what is needed.

29
00:02:19,371 --> 00:02:24,514
Coach: Sometimes it is one precise question; sometimes it is a short confirmation;

30
00:02:24,514 --> 00:02:28,371
Coach: sometimes it is a relevant contribution of your own.

31
00:02:28,721 --> 00:02:30,435
Coach: This lesson ends here.

32
00:02:30,435 --> 00:02:34,721
Coach: You can revisit the examples or repeat the private rehearsal.

33
00:02:34,721 --> 00:02:37,721
Coach: Carry one check into the next discussion:

34
00:02:37,721 --> 00:02:44,578
Coach: before replying, be clear about which part of the other person's contribution you are responding to.
